{{define "title"}}Open an .mpp file without Microsoft Project | MPP Viewer{{end}}

{{define "main"}}
<main class="landing centered" id="landing">
  {{with .Flash}}<p class="flash">{{.}}</p>{{end}}

  <h1>Open an .mpp file without Microsoft Project</h1>
  <p class="lead">You were sent a schedule, you do not have Microsoft Project, and you need to read the thing now. Drop the file below and the plan appears - no install, no licence, no signup.</p>

  <div class="drop" id="drop" data-max-upload="{{.MaxUpload}}" data-open-saved>
    <input type="file" id="file" accept=".mpp,.xml,.mpx,.mpd,.xer" hidden>
    <p class="drop__hint">Drop a file here or <label for="file" class="link">pick one from disk</label></p>
    <p class="drop__promise">Your file is never saved. It is read in memory and gone as soon as the chart is drawn.</p>
    <ul class="drop__terms">
      <li>.mpp .xml .mpx .mpd .xer</li>
      <li>up to {{.MaxUploadLabel}}</li>
      <li>no signup</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p class="drop__error is-hidden" id="error"></p>

  <section class="prose">
<h2>What is actually inside an .mpp file</h2>

<p>
  An <code>.mpp</code> file is a binary document. Not text, not a zip archive, not a database you
  can point something at - a structured binary container that Microsoft Project writes for its
  own use, holding tasks, calendars, resources, links between tasks and a great deal of internal
  bookkeeping that only means anything to the program that wrote it.
</p>

<p>
  It is also not one format but several. The layout changed across product generations, and a
  file saved by Project 2003 is put together differently from one saved by Project 2021. Anything
  claiming to read <code>.mpp</code> files has to understand all of them, which is most of the
  reason there are so few tools that do.
</p>

<p>
  One consequence is worth knowing, because it explains a lot of frustration: the format is
  <strong>readable but not writable</strong>. There is no library anywhere that can produce a
  valid <code>.mpp</code> file. Only Microsoft Project writes them. So every tool you find will
  read the file and hand you something else - a chart, a spreadsheet, a PDF - and none of them
  will offer to save your changes back.
</p>

<h2>Why a text editor shows garbage</h2>

<p>
  Almost everyone tries this first, and it is a sensible thing to try. Opening the file in
  Notepad or VS Code produces a wall of unreadable characters with occasional recognisable
  fragments - a task name, a resource, the name of the person who saved it. Those fragments are
  real, which is what makes it tempting to keep digging.
</p>

<p>
  It never pays off. What you are seeing is text stored inside binary records, stripped of the
  structure that gives it meaning. The editor has no idea which string is a task name and which
  is a leftover, no idea that task 47 comes after task 12, and no access to the dates at all -
  those are numbers, not text, and they do not survive the trip through a text editor even to be
  looked at.
</p>

<p>
  Renaming the file changes nothing. <code>.xlsx</code> and <code>.docx</code> really are zip
  archives you can unpack, so the instinct is well earned - but <code>.mpp</code> is not one of
  them, and renaming it to <code>.zip</code> or <code>.xml</code> just produces an archive that
  will not open.
</p>

<h2>The options, and what each one costs</h2>

<ul class="facts">
  <li>
    <strong>Buy Microsoft Project.</strong> Solves it completely, at a price that is difficult to
    justify for reading one file someone emailed you. It is also Windows-only, which is a
    dead end outright if you are on a Mac.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Ask the sender to export.</strong> Free and often the right answer - a PDF or a
    spreadsheet from their copy of Project costs them a minute. The catch is that it costs
    <em>them</em> a minute, it puts you back in the queue behind their other work, and what comes
    back is a flat snapshot you cannot expand, collapse or search.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Install a desktop viewer.</strong> Several exist. You are installing a program with
    permanent access to your machine in order to read one attachment, most of them are Windows-only,
    and the free ones tend to become paid ones at the point where you have already committed.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>An online converter.</strong> Closest to what you want, and the category to be
    careful in: many quietly keep an indefinite copy of whatever you upload, which for a project
    schedule means client names, staff names and internal dates sitting on someone else's disk.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>What happens here instead</h2>

<p>
  The file is read in memory and never written to disk or to a database. There is no copy to
  expire, no retention window to trust and nothing to ask us to delete later - without an
  account, nothing about the plan survives the request that carried it. Reload the page and it is
  genuinely gone.
</p>

<p>
  What you get back is the schedule as a chart you can work with: the task hierarchy with phases
  you can collapse, dependency arrows, the critical path, non-working days shaded from the
  project calendar, and a click on any bar for its dates, progress, predecessors, resources and
  notes. If what you actually need is <a href="/mpp-to-excel">the numbers in a spreadsheet</a>, that route exists too.
</p>

<h2>A picture of the plan is not the plan</h2>

<p>
  Worth separating out, because the online viewers in this category mostly land in the same
  place: they render the schedule as pages you scroll through and offer a PDF at the end. That
  solves the narrow problem of seeing the file at all, which is real. It leaves the wider one
  untouched.
</p>

<p>
  Think about why the file was sent to you. Almost nobody needs to admire a schedule. They need
  to find the one task their own work hangs off, see what it waits for, work out whether the date
  they were given has any slack behind it, and find the person whose name is against it. Every
  one of those is a question, and a page image cannot answer questions - it has no notion of what
  a task is, so there is nothing to click, nothing to fold away and no way to change the scale
  when the plan runs two years and the page shows you three weeks.
</p>

<p>
  What loads below keeps the structure instead of flattening it: four time scales, phases that
  collapse, dependency arrows you can follow, a highlighted critical path, a searchable task
  table, and full detail on any task you click. It also does not ration you - no counter limiting
  you to a few files a day, which matters more than it sounds when the plan arrives three times
  in a week with corrections.
</p>

<h2>Common questions</h2>

<dl class="faq">
  <dt>Can I just rename the .mpp file to .txt or .xml?</dt>
  <dd>No. Renaming changes nothing about the contents. An .mpp file is a binary document, not text in disguise, so a text editor will only ever show fragments of task names floating in unreadable data.</dd>

  <dt>Is an .mpp file a zip archive?</dt>
  <dd>No. Some Microsoft formats such as .xlsx and .docx are zip archives you can unpack, which is why people try it. The .mpp format is not one of them; it is a structured binary document and unpacking it does nothing.</dd>

  <dt>What about a .xml file from Microsoft Project?</dt>
  <dd>That is Project XML, also called MSPDI. It is genuinely text and opens here the same way an .mpp does. If the sender has not sent the file yet, asking for XML costs them one extra step in the Save As dialog.</dd>

  <dt>Is a free viewer safer than uploading the file?</dt>
  <dd>It is a fair question to ask. Here the file is read in memory and never written to disk or to a database, and without an account nothing about it survives the request. A desktop viewer, by contrast, is an installed program with permanent access to your machine.</dd>

</dl>

